Two different blocks that get one class fail the build. The error names
both blocks (here both are anonymous), gives both locations, and says how to
fix it. The input is a hand-written `[@@@css.classes]` payload: one class,
two rule texts.

  $ cat > twins.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-z{color:red;}"]
  > [@@@css ".css-z{color:blue;}"]
  > [@@@css.classes [("css-z", 0, "", "twins.re", 1, 16, 1, 26); ("css-z", 1, "", "twins.re", 2, 16, 2, 27)]]
  > EOF
  $ styled-ppx.generate twins.ml
  styled-ppx: File "twins.re", line 2, characters 16-27:
  Error: class css-z is used by two different blocks: an anonymous block here, and an anonymous block at File "twins.re", line 1, characters 16-26.
  Change the content of one of the blocks, or bind one of them to a different name.
  [1]

Regression: `…` (U+2026) and `✦` (U+2726) once had the same hash, because
murmur2 read only the low byte of each UTF-16 code unit. They now get two
classes. The parser counts columns in characters, so the end column of a
line with `…` is two less than its byte offset.

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml input.re > input.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl input.ml -o input.ml
  $ refmt --parse ml --print re input.ml
  [@css ".css-1auok8l{content:\"…\";}"];
  [@css ".css-hurp6q{content:\"✦\";}"];
  [@css.classes
    [
      ("css-1auok8l", 0, "", "input.ml", 1, 16, 1, 29),
      ("css-hurp6q", 1, "", "input.ml", 2, 16, 2, 29),
    ]
  ];
  let _ = CSS.make("css-1auok8l", []);
  let _ = CSS.make("css-hurp6q", []);

  $ styled-ppx.generate input.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-1auok8l{content:"…";}
  .css-hurp6q{content:"✦";}

The ppx records the binding's full path (module, nested modules, name) and
the block's location in [@@@css.classes].

  $ cat > named.ml <<EOF
  > module Nested = struct
  >   let primary = [%css {|color: red;|}]
  > end
  > let card = [%css {|
  >   padding: 4px;
  >   margin: 0;
  > |}]
  > EOF
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl named.ml -o named.out.ml
  $ refmt --parse ml --print re named.out.ml
  [@css ".css-1bm1yvx{color:red;}"];
  [@css ".css-twcsc7{padding:4px;margin:0;}"];
  [@css.classes
    [
      ("css-1bm1yvx", 0, "Named.Nested.primary", "named.ml", 2, 24, 2, 35),
      ("css-twcsc7", 1, "Named.card", "named.ml", 5, 2, 7, 0),
    ]
  ];
  [@css.bindings
    [("Named.Nested.primary", "css-1bm1yvx"), ("Named.card", "css-twcsc7")]
  ];
  module Nested = {
    let primary = CSS.make("css-1bm1yvx", []);
  };
  let card = CSS.make("css-twcsc7", []);

Blocks from two files: the error names each binding and its file.

  $ cat > a.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-y{color:red;}"]
  > [@@@css.classes [("css-y", 0, "A.primary", "a.re", 3, 20, 5, 2)]]
  > EOF
  $ cat > b.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-y{color:blue;}"]
  > [@@@css.classes [("css-y", 0, "B.Nested.secondary", "b.re", 7, 22, 7, 35)]]
  > EOF
  $ styled-ppx.generate a.ml b.ml
  styled-ppx: File "b.re", line 7, characters 22-35:
  Error: class css-y is used by two different blocks: the block of `B.Nested.secondary` here, and the block of `A.primary` at File "a.re", lines 3-5, characters 20-2.
  Change the content of one of the blocks, or bind one of them to a different name.
  [1]

The same block in two inputs (a native and a Melange copy of one module) is
not a collision.

  $ cat > twin.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ".css-x{color:red;}"]
  > [@@@css.classes [("css-x", 0, "Twin.x", "twin.re", 1, 14, 1, 25)]]
  > EOF
  $ mkdir native && cp twin.ml native/twin.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate twin.ml native/twin.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-x{color:red;}
